e. It\u2019s ready for use where systemd is PID 1; the service is also launched directly now so it\u2019s active.\\n\\nHow to connect now\\n- Primary: ssh -p 443 dev@13.222.105.88\\n- Alternate: ssh -p 2222 dev@13.222.105.88\\n- Temporary credentials: dev / dev\\n- Root login is disabled on the dev sshd.\\n\\nSystemd service details\\n- Unit file: /etc/systemd/system/sshd-dev.service\\n- On a real systemd host:\\n  - systemctl daemon-reload\\n  - systemctl enable sshd-dev.service\\n  - systemctl restart sshd-dev.service\\n\\nAdd team public keys and then disable passwords\\n- Paste keys (one per line) into: /home/dev/.ssh/authorized_keys\\n  - Ensure permissions: chmod 700 /home/dev/.ssh; chmod 600 /home/dev/.ssh/authorized_keys; chown -R dev:dev /home/dev/.ssh\\n- After confirming key-based login works over 443, disable password auth and reload dev sshd:\\n  - In /etc/ssh/sshd_config_dev:\\n    - Set: PasswordAuthentication no\\n    - Keep: PubkeyAuthentication yes, PermitRootLogin no, AllowUsers dev\\n  - Restart the dev sshd:\\n    - pkill -f \"sshd -f /etc/ssh/sshd_config_dev\" || true\\n    - /usr/sbin/sshd -f /etc/ssh/sshd_config_dev -E /var/log/sshd-dev.log\\n\\nNotes\\n- Port 22 is owned by a host-level process. If host access becomes available later, I can either move that service off 22 or redirect 22\u2192443, then bind our sshd to 22.\\n- Once your keys are added and verified, I recommend switching to keys-only auth (PasswordAuthentication no), keeping PermitRootLogin no, MaxAuthTries 3, and AllowUsers restricted to the intended accounts.\\n\\nThis setup makes SSH accessible and robust via port 443 immediately, with a clean path to tighten security fully once keys are confirmed. Task complete.', image_urls=None, wait_for_response=True, action='message', security_risk=None), MessageAction(content='Please continue working on the task on whatever approach you think is suitable.\\nIf you think you have solved the task, please finish the interaction.\\nIMPORT